Match the window border interlock to the style

QtCurveConfig::windowBorder_menuColorChanged() clears the titlebar
only box each time the user ticks the menubar colour box. The style
keeps both bits. qtcCheckConfig() drops the menubar colour bit beside
a blended titlebar, or beside a menubar shade of the window border,
and beside nothing else. The dialog throws a live setting away. The
rule fails as an exclusion as well. The other click order leaves both
boxes ticked, and the page writes a window border of 3.

The same handler leaves the blend box ticked, and that pair is the one
the style does repair. The user ticks the menubar colour box, the file
keeps the bit, and the next read drops it.

Clear the blend box instead of the titlebar only box.
windowBorder_blendChanged() already holds that pair in the other
direction, so each combination that the page writes now survives a
load and an Apply.

The change sits on the branch work/dialog-state of the local tree, as commit 9 of 12.
A new merge request on system/qtcurve takes that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp b/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
index 8a247d1e..5ab86a9c 100644
--- a/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
+++ b/qt6/config/qtcurveconfig.cpp
@@ -1572,9 +1572,12 @@ void QtCurveConfig::windowBorder_blendChanged()
 
 void QtCurveConfig::windowBorder_menuColorChanged()
 {
+    // qtcCheckConfig() drops the menubar colour beside a blended titlebar, and
+    // beside a menubar shade of the window border. The style keeps the menubar
+    // colour beside the titlebar only colour.
     if(windowBorder_menuColor->isChecked())
     {
-        windowBorder_colorTitlebarOnly->setChecked(false);
+        windowBorder_blend->setChecked(false);
         if(SHADE_WINDOW_BORDER==shadeMenubars->currentIndex())
             shadeMenubars->setCurrentIndex(SHADE_NONE);
     }
